      "q": "71. What is the maximum concentration of equimolar solutions of ferrous sulphate and sodium sulphide so that when mixed in equal volumes, there is no precipitation of iron sulphide?\n(For iron sulphide, $\\left. {{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}} = 6.3\\quad {{10}^{ - 18}}} \\right)$",
      "a": "Let the maximum concentration of each solution be ${\\text{xmol}}/{\\text{L}}$. After mixing, the volume of the concentrations of each solution will be reduced to half i.e., ${\\text{x}}/2$.\n$\\therefore \\left[ {FeS{O^4}} \\right] = \\left[ {N{a_2}S} \\right] = \\dfrac{x}{2}M$\nThen, $\\left[ {F{e^{2 + }}} \\right] = \\left[ {FeS{O^4}} \\right] = \\dfrac{x}{2}M$\nAlso, $\\left[ {{S^{2 - }}} \\right] = \\left[ {N{a_2}S} \\right] = \\dfrac{x}{2}M$\n$Fe{S_{(x)}} \\leftrightarrow Fe_{(aq)}^{2 + } + S_{(aq)}^{2 - }$\n${{K_{sp}} = \\left[ {F{e^{2 + }}} \\right]\\left[ {{S^{2 - }}} \\right]} $\n${6.3 \\times {{10}^{ - 18}} = \\left( {\\dfrac{x}{2}} \\right)\\left( {\\dfrac{x}{2}} \\right)} $\n${\\dfrac{{{x^2}}}{4} = 6.3 \\times {{10}^{ - 18}}} $\n${ \\Rightarrow x = 5.02 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}} $\nIf the concentrations of both solutions are equal to or less than $5.02 \\times {10^{ - 9}}{\\text{M}}$, then there will be no precipitation of iron sulphide."
